WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.997
Acceleration support determines whether local AI feels interactive or merely possible.

00:00:03.997 --> 00:00:08.956
NVIDIA hardware commonly uses CUDA, a mature compute platform supported by many AI runtimes.

00:00:08.956 --> 00:00:13.561
AMD workflows may use ROCm where the operating system and GPU are supported,

00:00:13.561 --> 00:00:17.458
while Vulkan backends can provide broader cross-vendor acceleration for compatible tools.

00:00:17.458 --> 00:00:21.000
Support varies by model, driver, runtime build, and operating system,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.897
so a GPU brand alone does not guarantee a working path.

00:00:24.897 --> 00:00:27.377
Think of the stack as a chain.

00:00:27.377 --> 00:00:30.565
The operating system recognizes the GPU through its driver.

00:00:30.565 --> 00:00:34.107
The runtime must be compiled for a compatible acceleration backend.

00:00:34.107 --> 00:00:39.421
That backend must support the GPU architecture and the numerical formats used by the model.

00:00:39.421 --> 00:00:43.318
If any link is incompatible, the application may fail to load,

00:00:43.318 --> 00:00:47.569
silently run on the CPU, or offload only part of the workload.

00:00:47.569 --> 00:00:51.820
Confirm acceleration through runtime logs and GPU utilization rather than assuming that

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.299
installation success means the GPU is active.

00:00:54.299 --> 00:00:58.550
GPU offload places model layers or operations on the graphics processor while

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:02.093
retaining the remainder in system memory and on the CPU.

00:01:02.093 --> 00:01:05.989
Full offload is usually faster when the model fits in VRAM.

00:01:05.989 --> 00:01:08.823
Partial offload can make a larger model usable,

00:01:08.823 --> 00:01:11.657
but data movement and CPU computation reduce speed.

00:01:11.657 --> 00:01:14.846
CPU-only inference remains a valuable fallback for troubleshooting, embeddings,

00:01:14.846 --> 00:01:18.034
and smaller models, though interactive generation is typically slower.

00:01:18.034 --> 00:01:19.097
Version discipline matters.

00:01:19.097 --> 00:01:22.639
Record the known-good GPU driver, acceleration runtime, and application build.

00:01:22.639 --> 00:01:25.119
Do not update every layer at once.

00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:26.890
When a change is necessary,

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:30.787
verify one representative model before modifying the rest of the environment.

00:01:30.787 --> 00:01:35.038
A slightly older stable stack that consistently uses acceleration is more useful

00:01:35.038 --> 00:01:38.580
than a newer combination whose compatibility has not been proven.
